.TH "KOSH-SWITCHING" "5" "October 27, 2026" "kosh" "File Formats Manual"
.SH Name
kosh\-switching \- switch from Bash to Koshka
.SH Description
Koshka\[cq]s default \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood checks shell code before it
runs and refuses code with errors.
Try Koshka in a new terminal before changing your login shell.
Keep your current Bash session open while you check your startup files.
.SH Keep your Bash setup
Start an interactive Koshka session with both startup moods.
.IP
.EX
kosh \-L kosh,bash
.EE
.PP
Koshka reads its own startup script and then your Bash startup files.
In an interactive session that is not a login shell, the Bash mood reads
the system Bash startup file and \f[CR]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R].
A login shell reads \f[CR]/etc/profile\f[R] and the first available Bash
login profile.
That profile must source \f[CR]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R] if you want its
aliases and functions in a login session.
.PP
The default \f[CR]kosh\f[R] startup mood reads
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R], or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R] when
\f[CR]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is unset or is not an absolute path.
On Unix, the superuser skips this user configuration directory.
The default startup mood does not automatically read
\f[CR]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R].
.PP
To keep both startup moods for future sessions, run this command inside
Koshka.
.IP
.EX
koshconf set init_moods kosh,bash \-\-persist
.EE
.PP
If you used \f[CR]koshconf create kosh\f[R], inspect the generated
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] first.
That preset already sources Bash startup files.
Choose one method to avoid running your Bash startup files twice.
.SH Run Bash scripts
Use the Bash mood when the default strict mood refuses a script that you
want to run with Bash behavior.
.IP
.EX
kosh \-\-mood bash script.bash
.EE
.PP
This selects Koshka\[cq]s Bash compatibility mood.
You can also keep Bash installed and run a script with
\f[CR]bash script.bash\f[R].
Existing \f[CR]#!/bin/bash\f[R] shebangs still select Bash.
.SH Change your login shell
On a Unix system with \f[CR]chsh\f[R], first find the installed binary
and check that you can run it.
.IP
.EX
command \-v kosh
kosh \-\-version
.EE
.PP
Add that absolute path to \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] if it is not already
listed.
Run these commands from your current Bash session.
.IP
.EX
kosh_path=\[dq]$(command \-v kosh)\[dq]
grep \-Fx \[dq]$kosh_path\[dq] /etc/shells \f[B]||\f[R] printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[dq]$kosh_path\[dq] \f[B]|\f[R] sudo tee \-a /etc/shells
chsh \-s \[dq]$(command \-v kosh)\[dq]
.EE
.PP
Sign out and sign in again to use the new login shell.
A terminal application with its own shell command may also need its
settings updated.
Windows does not use \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] or \f[CR]chsh\f[R].
Set Koshka as a terminal profile there.
.SH Switch back
Keep Bash installed until you have finished switching.
From a Bash session, check that its absolute path is listed in
\f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R], then select it.
.IP
.EX
chsh \-s \[dq]$(command \-v bash)\[dq]
.EE
.PP
Sign out and sign in again.
Restore any terminal profile that starts Koshka directly before removing
the binary.
.SH Remove Koshka
If you installed Koshka with a package manager, remove it with that
package manager.
For example, a Homebrew installation can be removed with
\f[CR]brew uninstall kosh\f[R].
.PP
The standalone Unix installer normally puts the binary in
\f[CR]\[ti]/.local/bin\f[R].
When run as root, it uses \f[CR]/usr/local/bin\f[R].
A custom installation may use a different directory.
Check \f[CR]command \-v kosh\f[R] before removing the installed binary.
Remove its entry from \f[CR]/etc/shells\f[R] after selecting another
login shell.
.PP
The Windows installer normally puts \f[CR]kosh.exe\f[R] in
\f[CR]%LOCALAPPDATA%\[rs]kosh\[rs]bin\f[R].
Close Koshka sessions, remove the installed binary, and remove that
directory from your user \f[CR]PATH\f[R] if you added it during setup.
Keep any configuration or history files that you want to reuse.
.SH See also
The kosh(1) shell reference covers shell commands and invocation.
The kosh(5) configuration manual covers options and startup settings.
